<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine VII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/905">905</a>–959</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, Constantine Porphyrogennetos · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus was Byzantine emperor, nominally from 913 and in real power from 945 to 959. A scholar-emperor, he sponsored the treatise known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a>, written for his son, which describes the peoples around the empire, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, Hungarians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and Rus'. It is a main source for the early history of many Balkan and steppe peoples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-vii.jpg" alt="Constantine VII" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wooofer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantine_VII_Porphyrogenitus_(2).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Constantine was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 17 or 18 May 905, the son of Emperor Leo VI by his fourth wife, Zoe Karbonopsina, a marriage the church regarded as uncanonical. To help legitimise him, his mother gave birth to him in the Purple Room of the imperial palace, where legitimate children of reigning emperors were normally born, hence the epithet Porphyrogennetos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/born-in-the-purple">born in the purple</a>. His father and uncle raised him to the throne as a two-year-old child on 15 May 908. In early 913 his dying uncle Alexander appointed a seven-man regency council headed by Patriarch Nicholas Mystikos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Most of Constantine's reign was dominated by others. The patriarch made an unpopular peace with Tsar Simeon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and Zoe drove him out of the regency, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> defeated her general Leo Phokas in 917. In March 919 the admiral Romanos Lekapenos replaced her as regent and married his daughter Helena to Constantine; he became co-emperor on 17 December 920, and Constantine was eclipsed by a senior emperor. Constantine spent those years studying the ceremonies of the court.</p><p>Romanos was deposed by his sons in December 944, and Constantine became sole emperor on 27 January 945, aged 39. He left government largely to bureaucrats, generals and his wife Helena. In 947 he ordered the return of peasant lands without compensation. A fleet sent against the Arab corsairs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in 949 failed, but in the east Nikephoros Phokas took Adata in 958 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John Tzimiskes</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> a year later. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olga-of-kiev">Olga of Kiev</a>, regent of the Rus', visited him in 957 and was baptised. Constantine died on 9 November 959 and his son Romanos II succeeded him; it was rumoured that he had been poisoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Constantine was a writer and scholar who gathered educated men round him. He wrote or commissioned the Geoponika, a compilation of earlier agronomic works; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-ceremoniis">De Ceremoniis</a>, on court ceremonies; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a>, advice for his son on running the empire and dealing with its enemies; and the Excerpta Historica, selections from ancient historians, of which only six of fifty-three titles survive. A history praising his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> is also attributed to him, though his authorship is uncertain. John Julius Norwich calls him the Scholar Emperor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constantine VII” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus</b>, writing about 950, described Patzinakia as stretching west to the Siret River and divided into eight provinces under the same number of great princes, four tribes on each side of the Dnieper, reflecting the Turkic division into left and right.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pechenegs">Pechenegs · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>For nearly a thousand years Hagia Sophia was the cathedral of Constantinople, the seat of its patriarch and a principal setting for imperial ceremonies such as coronations, which the emperor <b>Constantine VII</b> described in his Book of Ceremonies.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The emperor (basileus) was held to be chosen by God and to be the living law (nomos empsychos), and his court, whose ceremonies <b>Constantine VII</b> had set down in the Book of Ceremonies, was meant to mirror the order of heaven.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Romanos I Lekapenos, an admiral of humble origin and father-in-law of the young <b>Constantine VII</b>, was crowned co-emperor and governed in his place.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-17">17 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Byzantine emperor Romanos I Lekapenos, regent and senior co-ruler for <b>Constantine VII</b> since 920, was arrested by two of his sons and forced to abdicate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-20">20 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At the urging of their sister, the Augusta Helena, a coup removed the co-emperors Stephen and Constantine Lekapenos from power and restored sole imperial authority to <b>Constantine VII</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-27">27 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=940" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Constantine VII"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=940">What was here in 940 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
